        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "@justinmk \r\nHappy to loop in @bfredl and GUI-client authors — agreed this deserves careful review.\r\n\r\n**On overlap with events since #8707:** \r\nI went through them. The existing `win_*`/`grid_*` events (including the post-#8707 additions like `win_viewport`, `win_viewport_margins`, `win_extmark`) are all *state broadcasts* — nvim computes the layout and notifies the UI of the result. The events in this PR are the opposite direction: they *delegate a \r\nwindow-management action* to an external UI (e.g. `win_split`, `win_move`, `win_resize`; `win_move_cursor` is actually a request that returns a value). So there's no literal duplication, but two adjacencies are worth reconciling:\r\n\r\n- `win_resize(win, grid, width, height)` sits next to the existing `grid_resize(grid, width, height)` — same-ish args, opposite direction. Open to renaming to avoid confusion.\r\n- `win_move(win, grid, flags)` is adjacent to the `win_pos`/`win_float_pos`/`win_external_pos` family.\r\n\r\n**On granularity / combining:** \r\nI think a few can be merged, and I'm happy to:\r\n\r\n- fold `win_resize_equal` into `win_resize` as a mode/flag (it's just the parameterless special case), and\r\n- potentially unify `win_move`/`win_exchange`/`win_rotate` under a single \"rearrange\" event with an op field.\r\n\r\nThat said, you raised the ergonomics tradeoff yourself — if you and the GUI authors feel the finer granularity is more useful to implement against, I'm equally happy to keep them separate. I'll defer to what's easiest for client authors to consume.\r\n\r\nWill keep the PR rebased regularly regardless of review cadence. Thanks for the guidance!\r\n",
